From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:40:22 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] phy: apple: atc: take the USB controller through hibernation
 for a sleep

Before a deep sleep the controller stopped as for a cable disconnect,
and every USB device was gone after the wake. macOS's host goes to
Sleep, not Off, and the hub and its devices keep their slots:

- xHCI halts and saves its state.
- The controller's power control takes the core into hibernation: the D3
  request, wait for VVDD_USB_OFF, clear it, arm the two wake events,
  wait for SLEEP.
- The core's reset is held without the clamp, which only its Off path
  sets.
- The eUSB2 PHY goes to its sleep state, reference clock gated and APB
  in reset.
- ATC0_USB and ATC0_COMMON go off. ATC0_USB_AON stays on.
- At the wake: the eUSB2 PHY out of its sleep state, D0 requested, the
  reset released, wait for D0_ON, clear the events. xHCI restores its
  state and the root port resumes.

Do that from the PHY's noirq callbacks. The PHY already drives this
controller's reset in the PIPE handler, owns the USB2 PHY, and suspends
after the controller as its supplier, so xHCI has saved its state by
then. ATC0_COMMON takes the common block's setup with it, so the block
is set up again in the mode it had, USB2 or USB3, without the
controller's reset or a USB2 PHY power cycle, before the controller's
D0; a USB3 setup that fails there falls back to USB2. The secondary eUSB2
bank, next to the controller and on a rail that isn't known, gets its
defaults only after D0. The sleep controller is told to keep
ATC0_USB_AON for that sleep.

The SuperSpeed lanes stay as they are, as macOS leaves them: when its
T8140 host goes to Sleep, setUSB3Mode locks the TX-detect/RX state in
the power control and the PIPE interface in the PIPE handler, two
handshakes whose timeouts it only logs, and puts the USB3 PHY in its
sleep power level (25G83, read statically). Here USB3 sleeps in place
like USB2: at noirq, with xHCI halted, the PIPE is parked on the dummy
backend just before the hibernation, and a lock the halted controller
doesn't acknowledge is logged and the PIPE parked unlocked, as for a
stopped controller. The commit takes the lanes down, and the wake sets
them up in USB3 again with the PIPE still parked. The controller's glue
routes the PIPE to them when it sets the core up, before xHCI starts, so
no Type-C mode change and no SuperSpeed port power cycle follow. On
macOS too, the raise from Sleep to the saved USB3 mode is the
TX-detect/RX lock handshake and the USB3 PHY's power level (setUSB3Mode,
read statically). The controller's glue asks for all this with
apple_atcphy_sleep_prepare() before the freezer;
apple_atcphy_sleep_result() says how the wake went, and
apple_atcphy_sleep_finish() checks the SuperSpeed side against the mux
afterwards. A mux update runs then only if the mux changed during the
sleep, the core kept its registers, or the setup at the wake failed.
DisplayPort on the port leaves the controller stopping for the sleep as
before. A mode change in between refuses the sleep. A hibernation that
doesn't complete refuses that sleep, and only such failures of the PHY's
own make the controller stop for the later sleeps: a host its glue set
up again or restarted after a sleep hibernates again at the next one.

The block's always-on side can lose its power in the sleep anyway: its
power control then reads all zeros at the wake and never raises D0_ON.
The wake doesn't wait for it then, and redoes what the reset's release
and the probe do for the rest of the block: the core's clamp released
and the USB2 PHY's host role set. Both are no-ops for a block that kept
its state.

A mux update decides it has work to do before it takes the USB locks,
and an update holds them for the whole of its change. One that waited
behind another for the same mode took the SuperSpeed port down and up
again and ran its own retrain. The port controller's revalidation after
a wake did that behind the glue's SuperSpeed restore, and held a quick
sleep's freezer (measured on a J700). An update now looks again once it
holds the locks, and leaves the port alone if the other one finished the
change. A KUnit case covers it.

A stopped T8140 controller no longer clocks the PIPE lock's
acknowledgment, so a stopped controller's PIPE is parked without the
lock, as on T8103 and T8122.

Measured on a J700: the block loses its always-on power in every sleep
so far, so the core comes back with its registers reset and its glue
sets it up again. Taking USB3 to USB2 and back for the sleep, as this
did first, cost 0.6 s of every sleep's entry, and waiting for a D0_ON
that couldn't come cost 200 ms of every wake. Setting the block up in
USB2 at the wake and bringing USB3 back with a whole mode change
afterwards took 1.8 s after every wake, most of it two retrains waiting
for a SuperSpeed link that wasn't there. With the mode set up again at
the wake, the SuperSpeed side is already in its mode once the system is
up.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>

[j604 backport: KUnit file only: the original forward-declares atcphy_test_count_refused_lock(), which Aurora defines with its legacy-PIPE tests (not in this tree), so the KUnit build failed with -Wundefined-internal. The helper is defined at that point instead, with the original body. atc.c and apple-atc.h as in the original. On J604 (T8132 PHY: no has_t8140_dp, no pwrctl) apple_atcphy_sleep_prepare() refuses, so the controller stops for a deep sleep; the mux update's second look under the locks applies to every generation.]
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
